-- facts/findings.rofl — the findings ledger. Prose narratives live in
-- docs/dogfood/; this file is the actionable index the engine replays at
-- session start. Discipline: rules/findings.rofl.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- open ---

-- Recorded 2026-08-30 while building examples/slop (SLOP). Both are open on
-- purpose: the first is a design conclusion the semiring layer should carry
-- in its own documentation, the second names a hole in an existing audit.

finding(f_semiring_fold_is_structural_not_semantic, insight).
recorded(f_semiring_fold_is_structural_not_semantic, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_semiring_fold_is_structural_not_semantic, doc).
finding_note(f_semiring_fold_is_structural_not_semantic,
  "SLOP was asked for an intervalSemiring in runtime/semirings.ts and the right answer turned out to be a reason NOT to write one, which is worth more than the instance: evaluateSemiring computes v(f) = plus over supports of (weight times product of premises), so ONE times combines the premises of every firing whatever rule fired it - and the support hypergraph does not record WHICH ARITHMETIC a firing performed, so for val(N,V) :- plus(N,A,B), val(A,X), val(B,Y), V is X+Y the fold cannot be + at a plus node and * at a times node; the fold therefore answers WHICH (provenance), HOW MANY (counting), HOW DEEP/CHEAP (tropical, viterbi) and HOW TRUSTED (bleep) and can NEVER answer WHAT NUMBER, which is why SLOP puts interval arithmetic in the RULES (iv/ivalue, ~50 rules) and uses the fold only for the three structural questions; a second, independent reason points the same way: general interval arithmetic is only SUB-distributive - a times (b plus c) CONTAINS (a times b) plus (a times c) and is generally strictly larger, with equality recovered only on non-negative intervals - so an interval instance would either fail test/semiring.test.ts's law checks or would have to carry a sign restriction that a financial model breaks on its first negative cash flow; the doc this demands is one paragraph at the top of runtime/semirings.ts saying what the fold is FOR, because the next person asked for a numeric carrier will otherwise write the instance and discover this the slow way").

finding(f_edb_typo_invisible_to_undefined_premise, pitfall).
recorded(f_edb_typo_invisible_to_undefined_premise, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_edb_typo_invisible_to_undefined_premise, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_edb_typo_invisible_to_undefined_premise,
  "found the hard way in examples/slop: the host emitted num_text(N, S) for a string literal inside a formula while slop.rofl declared edb(lit_text) and read lit_text(N, V) - two DIFFERENT names, one existing only as data and one only as a declaration, so undefined_premise[audit] stayed EMPTY (lit_text is edb-declared, so it is defined; num_text has base facts, so edb(num_text) is auto-emitted and it is defined too) and every IF whose condition compared against a text literal silently produced no value, on 295 IF calls in a real workbook; LIMITS.md already says the audit is a dictionary check on NAMES and not a coverage check, and this is the exact hole that leaves: a relation that is declared but never populated is invisible, and so is one that is populated but never read; the cheap catch is the mirror of the existing audit - a rule pack that declares edb(Rel) and receives NO facts for Rel, or receives facts for a relation no rule reads, should be flagged, which is one rule each over edb/1 plus the reflected reads_from; it is not free (a legitimately optional table is exactly 'declared but empty', which is why moot.rofl's cond_alt exists) so the flag has to be a warning with an explicit silencer rather than an error - the design question this demands is what that silencer looks like").


-- Recorded 2026-08-30 by the HONK/HUH sufficiency probe. Prose + measured
-- numbers: docs/dogfood/2026-08-30-honk-huh.md. Deliberately left OPEN: each
-- demands kernel work or a spec decision that this session did not take.

finding(f_main_guard_breaks_on_symlinked_tmp, pitfall).
recorded(f_main_guard_breaks_on_symlinked_tmp, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_main_guard_breaks_on_symlinked_tmp, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_main_guard_breaks_on_symlinked_tmp,
  "npm test is RED on main on macOS and green in CI, found while probing something else: runtime/pair.ts guards with path.resolve(process.argv[1]) === new URL(import.meta.url).pathname, but path.resolve does NOT follow symlinks while Node realpaths module URLs - os.tmpdir() on macOS is /var/folders/... symlinked to /private/var/folders/..., so inside the skill bundle built into a temp dir isMain is false, main() never runs, and the process exits 0 with EMPTY stdout; the smoke then reports the honest-looking but misleading 'init report lacks the no_go verdict' when the report was never generated at all; verified by reproduction (status 0, 0 stdout bytes, 0 stderr bytes) and by confirming the same bundle prints no_go when built under a non-symlinked path; fix is fs.realpathSync on both sides of the comparison; this is f_ci_is_the_referee inverted - here CI is the one that lies because Linux /tmp is not symlinked, so 'green in Actions' hid a guard that silently disables an entry point").
addressed_by(f_main_guard_breaks_on_symlinked_tmp, "runtime/pair.ts").

finding(f_test_pinned_census_forbids_open_findings, pitfall).
recorded(f_test_pinned_census_forbids_open_findings, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_test_pinned_census_forbids_open_findings, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_test_pinned_census_forbids_open_findings,
  "test/findings.test.ts pinned the literal ledger census ('nothing open, twenty-four settled' and /all 24 findings settled/), so recording ANY finding turned the suite red - a test forbidding exactly what CLAUDE.md's protocol permits (a finding left open deliberately), which pressures the next author to either not record the finding or to settle it dishonestly; replaced with the invariant actually worth pinning: every finding is exactly one of open or settled, every disposition names a declared finding (catching typo'd ids), and every OPEN finding appears in the rendered report").
addressed_by(f_test_pinned_census_forbids_open_findings, "test/findings.test.ts").

finding(f_viterbi_in_log_space, insight).
recorded(f_viterbi_in_log_space, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_viterbi_in_log_space,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-30 (choice delegated to the top level): Viterbi gets a LOG-SCALE integer carrier, not rationals-as-terms; probabilities multiply along a chain, and the kernel has no fractions at all (3/2 = 1) while even with floats a long chain underflows to zero and every path looks equally impossible - storing round(log(p) * 1e6) turns multiplication into addition, which needs only integers and does not collapse; the payoff that makes this better than a compromise is that log-space Viterbi IS the already-written tropical semiring with max instead of min, so it reuses that code path rather than adding new mathematics, and it lands in the BOUNDED discipline automatically because every log-probability is negative so going round a cycle only makes a path worse and the iteration settles by itself; probability zero is -infinity which is exactly the semiring zero; the honest cost, to be stated in the instance documentation like every other: rounding error accumulates LINEARLY in chain length rather than compounding, and two very close paths can tie; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a chain where the linear rounding accumulation FLIPS the best path relative to exact arithmetic, turning the documented cost from a tie into a wrong answer").
demands(f_viterbi_in_log_space, kernel_test).
addressed_by(f_viterbi_in_log_space, "runtime/semirings.ts").

finding(f_bun_runner_over_budget, pitfall).
recorded(f_bun_runner_over_budget, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_bun_runner_over_budget, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_bun_runner_over_budget,
  "both runners are green after the semiring layer landed - node 106 pass / 0 fail in 134s, bun 106 pass / 0 fail in 308s - but bun has crossed the 300-second hard budget the operating rules set, and it is 2.3x slower than node on the identical suite; this is the Gospriyomka failure mode in slow motion: a gate nobody will sit through gets switched off, and CLAUDE.md requires BOTH runners stay green, so the one people will actually skip is the one that silently stops being run; separately a measurement lesson - the first bun run reported 42 failures which were pure instrument error, because bare 'bun test' applies a 5-second default timeout while the project's own script is 'bun test --timeout 60000', so the correct invocation is npm run test:bun and never the bare command").
dismissed(f_bun_runner_over_budget, bun_is_ci_only).
finding(f_kill_is_not_cancel_for_an_agent, pitfall).
recorded(f_kill_is_not_cancel_for_an_agent, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_kill_is_not_cancel_for_an_agent,
  "measured the hard way across four rounds: a message to a subagent is delivered at its NEXT TOOL ROUND, so an agent blocked inside a long command cannot receive the message telling it to stop running that command - three polite countermands sat undelivered while three agents sat inside bun runs; worse, killing the child process does NOT cancel the intent, it looks to the agent like a FAILED RUN and triggers a retry, which is why a fourth bun started minutes after the third was killed; the closed loop was self-inflicted: the brief demanded bun, three agents ran it, load hit 111 on 4 cores, bun then took roughly three times its normal 295 s, and the agents were unreachable for the whole window; TWO OPERATIONAL RULES follow - to stop a long-running agent operation use TaskStop, not a message and not kill, because only TaskStop cancels the intent rather than the attempt; and check `ps` before composing a third polite message, since load average and a process list answer 'why is it not responding' in one second where inference does not; the load numbers also explain a measurement artefact worth remembering: the three example test files took 20 s under the pile-up and 8 s on a quiet box, so any timing taken during parallel agent work is inflated and must be re-taken before it is quoted").
addressed_by(f_kill_is_not_cancel_for_an_agent, "docs/dogfood/2026-08-30-honk-huh.md").

finding(f_enforced_a_rule_without_pricing_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_enforced_a_rule_without_pricing_it, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_enforced_a_rule_without_pricing_it,
  "caught by Vadim: 'you are slowing development down with slow tests, node is enough'; the top level took CLAUDE.md's line 'both runners must stay green: bun test too' literally, wrote 'npm test AND npm run test:bun both green' into all four subagent briefs, and then spent a turn PREDICTING that the new demos would push bun over the 300s budget - while bun already had its own CI job in .github/workflows/ci.yml, so nothing local was being protected at all; cost: about five minutes of dead verification per agent cycle, three agents in parallel; the class is not carelessness but enforcing a written rule without ever pricing the enforcement - and the sting is that the same CLAUDE.md carries the Gospriyomka lesson ('a check nobody will sit through gets switched off, and then its absence is invisible'), which the top level quoted several times this session while manufacturing exactly that check; FIXED AT THE SOURCE, not only in behaviour: CLAUDE.md now says the development loop is node only, names the measured 117s vs 295s, points at the CI job, and states explicitly that 'both runners green IN CI' is a different instruction from 'run both by hand' - the ambiguity in the original sentence is what a brief-level fix has to remove").
addressed_by(f_enforced_a_rule_without_pricing_it, "CLAUDE.md").


finding(f_source_must_stay_reviewable, pitfall).
recorded(f_source_must_stay_reviewable, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_source_must_stay_reviewable,
  "the semiring agent wrote a literal NUL byte into runtime/semirings.ts (a separator constant written as the raw character instead of the escape), and it was invisible to EVERY gate: tsc clean, its own 11 tests green, npm run grepcheck clean because that check scans src/ only - while git classified the TypeScript source as binary ('Bin 0 -> 7930 bytes', 'Binary files differ', numstat '- -'), so the file could not be diffed, reviewed or line-blamed; for a repo whose premise is auditable reasoning a source nobody can review is a defect regardless of whether it runs; it also silently broke grep, which is how it hid - probes came back empty and empty was read as 'nothing there' rather than 'the instrument refused'; fixed, and the gate that would have caught it now exists").
addressed_by(f_source_must_stay_reviewable, "scripts/text_check.ts").

finding(f_empty_output_is_not_a_measurement, pitfall).
recorded(f_empty_output_is_not_a_measurement, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_empty_output_is_not_a_measurement,
  "one class behind four separate top-level errors in a single session, two caught by hooks and two by Vadim: a negative or empty tool result was taken as a fact about the SUBJECT when it was a fact about the INSTRUMENT - empty grep read as 'no exports' (grep had refused a binary file), empty tail read as 'the run produced nothing' (it was still running), '$?' after a pipeline read as the command's status (it is the last stage's; zsh spells the array pipestatus), and 'cannot enumerate' read as 'cannot verify' (deciding it via SCC needs no enumeration at all); the corrective is a mandatory POSITIVE CONTROL - a negative result is not a measurement until the same instrument has been shown to produce a non-empty answer on a known-positive case, and 'not findable by method X' must name X and ask for a second method; recorded as RULE 2 in the global operating rules, and mechanised for one instance by scripts/text_check.ts whose own test plants the defect to prove the gate can say no - it rejected its own author on first run").
addressed_by(f_empty_output_is_not_a_measurement, "test/text-check.test.ts").

finding(f_infinite_is_decidable_not_enumerable, insight).
recorded(f_infinite_is_decidable_not_enumerable, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_infinite_is_decidable_not_enumerable, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_infinite_is_decidable_not_enumerable,
  "Vadim caught the top level asking the wrong question TWICE in a row - first 'is there a labelled dataset' instead of 'is the modelled thing executable', then 'can it be enumerated' instead of 'can it be DECIDED'; measured: why does NOT report cycles today (its [cycle] marker is a render guard against infinite recursion and never fired on the cyclic craft graph, because the tree follows the single first witness and reaches an axiom without revisiting), but Tarjan SCC over the support hypergraph names the cycle exactly and cheaply - craftable(heavy_oil/light_oil/petrol_gas) plus their three ok/1 facts, i.e. the petroleum cracking loop, while craftable(water/coal/crude_oil/plastic) sit outside every SCC and their counts are finite and safe to report; so 'infinitely many derivations' is DECIDABLE and independently checkable with any graph library, and the earlier claim that HONK's counting column is unverifiable in principle was wrong; the product consequence is the important half: 'infinite because THESE recipes form a cycle' is a finding about the domain the user can act on (accept infinity, count simple paths, or fix the model), and for access control an assume-role cycle is itself the security result - the SCC is the provenance of this unknown, exactly as the RIP decision requires; PRECISION: SCC membership implies an infinite count only for NON-IDEMPOTENT plus - the same scan finds SCCs in the kernel's own reach/stratum/sees, where a support cycle is harmless, so cycle membership must be interpreted through the instance's declared discipline or it cries wolf on every recursive Boolean program").

finding(f_fork_copies_the_whole_store, pitfall).
recorded(f_fork_copies_the_whole_store, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_fork_copies_the_whole_store, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_fork_copies_the_whole_store,
  "IFFY's first acceptance criterion is 'fork the rule set WITHOUT copying facts', and that is exactly what the kernel does not do: excise() calls store.clone(), and clone() is snapshot() -> JSON -> restore(), where restore() re-adds every fact through add() with its binary-search-plus-splice insert; measured clone cost 2000 facts/12ms, 8000/74ms, 20000/322ms - superlinear, same O(n^2) root cause as f_store_index_insert_quadratic, so ~100k facts is roughly 8 seconds PER FORK; IFFY wants to diff two fixpoints over a corpus of ten thousand past decisions and Tropical-search the minimal amendment, which means many forks, not one; the fix is a copy-on-write or layered store (a branch holding only its delta over a shared base) rather than a deep copy, and it would also make excise cheap - note excise's SEMANTICS are already right and already shipped, this is purely the cost of getting there").

finding(f_a_hypothesis_can_be_kept_structurally, insight).
recorded(f_a_hypothesis_can_be_kept_structurally, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_hypothesis_can_be_kept_structurally,
  "the DRIP demo answered a brief requirement in a stronger form than it was asked, and the technique generalises: DRIP.md says a detected metric RENAME (an old series vanished, a new one appeared with the same shape) is a GUESS and must be marked with a confidence rather than derived as a conclusion, and the brief warned that such a hypothesis tends to quietly harden into a verdict; instead of honouring that by convention or by a comment, the agent proved it STRUCTURALLY - a test asserts that no verdict relation depends on the rename guess ACCORDING TO THE KERNEL'S OWN DEPENDENCY GRAPH, and a second test deletes the rename evidence and asserts that not a single verdict changes; the pair means the property cannot be violated silently by a later edit, because adding such a dependency would fail the first test and any real reliance would fail the second; the general form worth carrying to every example that mixes derived conclusions with guessed inputs (AKA's bridge confidence, OOPS's citation types, JOPA's translation): a claim that something is 'only a hypothesis' is checkable against the dependency graph, and checking it beats asserting it").
addressed_by(f_a_hypothesis_can_be_kept_structurally, "examples/drip/").

finding(f_spat_inverts_the_domain_choice, insight).
recorded(f_spat_inverts_the_domain_choice, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_spat_inverts_the_domain_choice, doc).
finding_note(f_spat_inverts_the_domain_choice,
  "SPAT (a week of family scheduling) makes an argument no other spec in the set makes, and it inverts how demo domains are normally picked: constraint scheduling is NP-complete and on a large instance every result is SPLIT between the solver and the explanation with the explanation's share unprovable - on a family instance, a dozen events and one car, brute force is instant, so NOTHING visible can be attributed to the solver and the explanation's share is a hundred percent; the architecture follows directly and matches the discipline already seen twice in the set (LOOT refusing to build a package manager, SLOP refusing to build a spreadsheet editor): brute force or CP-SAT computes, ROFL explains, one constraint model feeding both; second novel element - CONSTRAINTS BELONG TO PEOPLE and some owners are OUTSIDE the household (the neighbours the morning chain runs through), so whynot answers not 'infeasible' but 'no, because this constraint, owned by that person, carries this chain', which turns the tool into an instrument for a NEGOTIATION rather than for planning, and is SUS/AKA machinery on a domestic domain; its ground truth is free and weekly - live the week - and the spec correctly says the DISAGREEMENTS are the valuable part, because a schedule that said it worked and did not almost always means the model diverged from reality when somebody agreed something in a messenger and never entered it, which is the honest applicability limit of the whole approach measured on a domain where the cost of being wrong is a spoiled evening rather than an incident; the spec nominates itself as the first YAK fragment for exactly that reason").
addressed_by(f_spat_inverts_the_domain_choice, "examples/spat/").

finding(f_ditto_settles_iffy_and_closes_yak, insight).
recorded(f_ditto_settles_iffy_and_closes_yak, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_ditto_settles_iffy_and_closes_yak, doc).
finding_note(f_ditto_settles_iffy_and_closes_yak,
  "DITTO arrives as the 16th spec and RESOLVES two questions the set had left hanging rather than adding a third: (1) IFFY explicitly asked 'separate example or mode' and DITTO answers it by being IFFY's domain - fork, two evaluations, diff with provenance, applied to two versions of CODE instead of two rule sets - so IFFY ships as a mode and DITTO is built as its application, not beside it; (2) YAK is closed by it - scanners accumulated ad hoc during real tasks become the DECLARED invariant set, YAK explains where they come from and DITTO explains why keeping them is a contract rather than hoarding; also gives Counting its FOURTH domain reading (see f_counting_reads_oppositely_by_domain): invariant formally preserved but having lost an independent path is not a violation, it is a future regression visible today, distinct from robustness in NOPE/OOPS, ambiguity in AKA and fragility in RIP; DITTO is also honest where it must be - program equivalence is undecidable, so it promises only that the DECLARED AND EXTRACTABLE invariant set survived, strictly weaker than the informal claim and strictly more useful because checkable; its ground truth is the cheapest in the whole set, real refactorings from repo history cross-checked against what the tests caught and what surfaced in production afterwards, which satisfies BOTH oracle axes at once; its whynot half became available this session with recursive whynot, and what still blocks it is IFFY's cheap fork (f_fork_copies_the_whole_store)").

finding(f_a_fabrication_ban_can_be_a_gate, insight).
recorded(f_a_fabrication_ban_can_be_a_gate, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_a_fabrication_ban_can_be_a_gate,
  "YAK.md states its central rule in prose - fragments must be RECORDS OF REAL RUNS, invented ones look more convincing than real ones and are forbidden for exactly that reason, and an empty cell is more honest than a filled one - and the spec goes further by disqualifying ITSELF, saying its own failure catalogue was reasoned rather than observed and must be checked against practice before building; the demo turned both into MECHANICAL GATES rather than honouring them by intention: tests assert that every fragment marked CONFIRMED has an artefact behind it, that NO PREDICTED MODE IS WRITTEN UP AS A FRAGMENT, that only the two labels meaning a real run happened are in use, that discarding is shown and not only retention, and that the mandatory fragments are present; result: 10 fragments, all CONFIRMED, zero predicted, with the modes that had no real instance listed as one-liners instead; THIS IS THE THIRD EXAMPLE TO MECHANISE AN ANTI-FABRICATION RULE without being asked - HUH and BLEEP both carry a test that the README and page quote real output verbatim - and it is the strongest form yet, because HUH's checks a transcript against a run while YAK's checks a CLAIM ABOUT PROVENANCE against an artefact; worth generalising: wherever a document asserts that its content came from somewhere, that assertion is testable, and testing it costs less than the credibility of the document").
addressed_by(f_a_fabrication_ban_can_be_a_gate, "examples/yak/").

finding(f_an_oracle_may_already_be_in_the_file, insight).
recorded(f_an_oracle_may_already_be_in_the_file, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_an_oracle_may_already_be_in_the_file,
  "the SLOP brief said to recompute the model with headless LibreOffice if it was available and to say so plainly if it was not; the agent found a better oracle that needed no second engine at all - an .xlsx stores the CACHED VALUE of every formula cell alongside the formula itself, so the file IS the ground truth, and the test 'the recomputation matches the values Excel cached, to the carrier unit' compares against what Excel actually computed when the model was last opened; consequences worth carrying: the oracle needs no install, no headless invocation and no version pinning, it travels with the fixture, and it is the real Excel rather than a compatible reimplementation; the same shape probably exists elsewhere - a build system's cache, a compiler's .d files, a database's materialised view - wherever a tool persists what it computed, the artefact carries its own oracle; SEPARATELY, the honest limit I asked for was found and stated rather than avoided: the test 'the acyclic evaluator is SILENT on a circular sheet, and that is correct' records that the oracle is deterministic only on acyclic sheets, because Excel's iterative calculation on circular ones has no convergence guarantee - which is precisely what SLOP exists to fix, so SLOP loses its oracle exactly on the models it is for; the demo answers that with 'the circular model converges, and the round is a derived fact' and 'when it does NOT converge, that is the answer, with the cell named', which is the contrast with the checkbox rather than a substitute oracle").
addressed_by(f_an_oracle_may_already_be_in_the_file, "examples/slop/").

finding(f_counting_reads_oppositely_by_domain, insight).
recorded(f_counting_reads_oppositely_by_domain, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_counting_reads_oppositely_by_domain, doc).
finding_note(f_counting_reads_oppositely_by_domain,
  "AKA's sharpest observation, and it belongs in the semiring instance documentation rather than in any one example: the SAME counting metric reads in OPPOSITE directions depending on domain - in NOPE (independent paths to a privilege) and OOPS (independent supports for a conclusion) many paths means ROBUST, while in AKA (mapping paths between two ontologies) many paths means AMBIGUOUS and is a defect; RIP adds a third reading where one path means fragile; so 'number of derivations' is not self-interpreting and an instance that ships without saying which reading applies invites the tool to report robustness where it should report ambiguity - the count is the same, the sign of its meaning is domain knowledge and must be stated per example; UPDATED 2026-08-30 after BLEEP and SPAT arrived: there are now FIVE readings, not three - robustness (NOPE, OOPS), ambiguity (AKA), LAUNDERABILITY (BLEEP - how many independent clean paths exist to wash a dirty derivation, zero meaning nothing can wash it), and FRAGILITY (SPAT - one way for a trip to happen is a single point of failure, and zero without a named person means the whole branch rests on them); SPAT reached the same conclusion independently and stated it harder than this finding did - the interpretation belongs to the INSTANCE, not to the metric - which is the formulation to put in the instance documentation").

finding(f_unknown_is_a_value_not_a_refusal, insight).
recorded(f_unknown_is_a_value_not_a_refusal, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_unknown_is_a_value_not_a_refusal, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_unknown_is_a_value_not_a_refusal,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-30, option b (alternating fixpoint / well-founded semantics) over local stratification: 'unknown is a completely valid value and we would rather show it explicitly and explain WHY it is unknown than blur it'; the reasoning that settled it is that in the two cheaper options the third category is obtained by SUBTRACTION (everything neither yes nor no), which silently merges 'genuinely depends on luck' with 'the engine ran out of budget' - and telling those two apart IS RIP's product, so an example computing its headline column as a leftover imitates that column rather than demonstrating it; consequence beyond RIP: unknown needs its OWN provenance, i.e. why unknown(X) must name the unfounded set / the circular dependency that left it undefined, not just report absence - a refusal explains nothing while a three-valued answer with a tree explains itself; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a case where the alternating fixpoint's unknown ALSO turns out to be obtained by subtraction in practice - unable to tell 'genuinely depends on luck' from 'the budget ran out' - since telling those apart is the entire reason it was chosen over the cheaper options").

finding(f_rename_leaks_into_the_firing_signature, pitfall).
recorded(f_rename_leaks_into_the_firing_signature, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_rename_leaks_into_the_firing_signature, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_rename_leaks_into_the_firing_signature,
  "the NOPE demo agent reported that a rule outside range restriction is silently demand-evaluated and that this corrupts the semiring fold while the Boolean answer stays right; the top level took the claim seriously, failed to reproduce the symptom it described (facts vanishing from the fold - they were present and annotated), and found the ACTUAL root cause, which is tighter and worse: THE CLAUSE-RENAMING COUNTER LEAKS INTO THE FIRING SIGNATURE; measured on a two-line fixture, a fact with exactly ONE derivation gets supportCount 2 under a demand-backed rule and 1 under the range-restricted form of the same logic, and the witnesses show why - the builtin premise records as 'pair(alice,?Z#0) = pair(alice,?Z#0)' from one call site and '?Z#1' from another, because renameClause gives each call site a fresh counter and PremRef.bi.desc is built from the resolved term, so an UNBOUND variable carries its call-site-specific renamed NAME into the description; sigOf then hashes two different strings for the same derivation and store.support records both as distinct firings; consequences: counting inflates in proportion to the number of call sites of a demand-backed relation, why can render duplicate branches, and any semiring folded over that hypergraph is folding over an inflated structure - all while the Boolean verdict stays correct, which is exactly what makes it invisible to behavioural tests; the fix is to canonicalise the description before it becomes a signature (number remaining variables positionally, or hash the resolved term structurally rather than by printed name); the agent's proposed mitigation is sound and worth keeping regardless as a demo-level hygiene assertion - any example folding a semiring should assert that no rule is unsafe and demandRels is empty - but it is a guard, not the repair; THIRD MEMBER of a family now: f_unknown_relation_silently_empty, f_counting_breaks_under_ticks and this one all have the same shape - the Boolean answer stays right and conceals that the semiring is folding over a different world than the one the verdicts describe").
addressed_by(f_rename_leaks_into_the_firing_signature, "test/firing-signature.test.ts").

finding(f_bridge_decl_is_about_rules_not_entities, insight).
recorded(f_bridge_decl_is_about_rules_not_entities, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_bridge_decl_is_about_rules_not_entities,
  "asked as the sharpest open question in the AKA brief and answered by a test rather than by prose: the kernel's own bridge_decl and the leak[audit] that rides on it are about RULES crossing perspectives - a rule whose head perspective differs from its body's - and NOT about entity-level mappings between two ontologies; so a data-level bridge (this billing account corresponds to that CRM customer, asserted by integration-team with confidence 0.8) is ordinary domain modelling and gets no kernel support beyond perspectives and asserted_by, which it uses well; the agent named the distinction instead of building a parallel mechanism and calling it the same thing, and separately verified the kernel's real one works, so both facts are now pinned; PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE for the specs that assume otherwise: AKA's 'the result is marked as having crossed a boundary' is achievable but must be modelled, and the demo's test 'the mark and the fold must agree: crossed exactly when certainty < 1' is what keeps the hand-built mark honest against the semiring that actually carries the confidence - a pattern worth copying wherever a demo maintains a flag that a fold could contradict").
addressed_by(f_bridge_decl_is_about_rules_not_entities, "examples/aka/").

finding(f_provenance_is_blind_through_negation, insight).
recorded(f_provenance_is_blind_through_negation, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_provenance_is_blind_through_negation, doc).
finding_note(f_provenance_is_blind_through_negation,
  "found and NAMED rather than worked around by the MOOT demo agent, as a passing test asserting the limitation: the provenance semiring cannot see through negation, because a conclusion that holds BECAUSE SOMETHING IS ABSENT has no source to point at - finite failure is not a fact and carries no annotation - so for MOOT the question 'which conditions carry this live flag' comes back with zero sources while the Boolean verdict 'the flag is live' is perfectly correct; the agent's own wording is exact: 'liveness rests on not dead_clause, and finite failure carries no annotation'; THE SCOPE IS WIDER THAN MOOT and that is why it is recorded here rather than left in one example's README - any verdict phrased as 'X unless Y' loses provenance the same way, which covers MOOT's five verdicts, NOPE's Deny-overrides-Allow, and OOPS's at_risk-stopping-at-grounded, i.e. three of the demos shipped today; it belongs to the same family as f_counting_breaks_under_ticks and f_rename_leaks_into_the_firing_signature - the Boolean answer stays right while the annotation describes something other than what was asked - but the mechanism is different and arguably not a defect at all: an empty provenance for a negation-carried conclusion is HONEST, since there genuinely is no positive source; what is missing is a way to say 'this rests on an absence, and here is the absence', which would be a whynot tree attached to a provenance term rather than a semiring change").
addressed_by(f_provenance_is_blind_through_negation, "examples/jopa/").

finding(f_counting_breaks_under_ticks, pitfall).
recorded(f_counting_breaks_under_ticks, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_counting_breaks_under_ticks, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_counting_breaks_under_ticks,
  "found by the OOPS demo agent, INDEPENDENTLY REPRODUCED by the top level on a minimal fixture: the kernel's own persistence idiom collides with non-idempotent semirings past tick 0; a carry rule (fact(X) @next :- fact(X)) makes every carried fact its OWN support one tick back, a self-loop in the support hypergraph, so the CLOSED counting instance multiplies by star(one) and answers 'infinitely many' for everything downstream - measured on a 7-fact program with an advancing clock: at tick 0 zero facts INFINITE and hop(a,c)=1, at tick 1 SIX OF SEVEN infinite including solo(z), a fact that cites nothing and has exactly one origin, with 3 facts literally self-supported; the agent measured the same on the real graph, 138 facts on a cycle in the ticked store against 93 un-ticked; THE ENGINE IS NOT WRONG - it answers 'how many derivations' about a support graph that now includes time travel - but the answer is useless for any domain question after tick 0, and this is a collision between two idioms the kernel itself teaches ('persistence is not a storage property', examples/counter.rofl and tm.rofl) rather than a defect in either; the demo's workaround is sound and worth keeping as the pattern: fold the semirings over an as-of store (retract the clock seed, assert the year, evaluate ONCE at tick 0) and prove it is the same world by checking the ticked and as-of stores agree on the domain facts - they did, 142 facts byte-identical; tropical and provenance are unaffected since neither closes cycles; options to decide between: fold over a tick-scoped view that excludes cross-tick support edges, mark carry-derivations so the fold can skip them, or document counting as an as-of-only query").

finding(f_past_tick_is_provenance_only, insight).
recorded(f_past_tick_is_provenance_only, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_past_tick_is_provenance_only, doc).
finding_note(f_past_tick_is_provenance_only,
  "found by the OOPS demo agent: 'the knowledge state at an earlier tick' is NOT queryable the way OOPS.md and several other specs assume - tick-scoped facts are DROPPED at the tick boundary, so standing at tick 5 there is no way to ask at_risk(P) as of tick 4 because those facts no longer exist; what survives is frozen derived_by/3, so the store still REPORTS what it derived at tick 4 alongside what it holds now - which answers the question at the granularity of 'what was derived' but not 'what could have been asked'; the two mechanisms are genuinely different and both are needed: simulateTo(year) runs the clock forward, exercises the carry rules and leaves frozen provenance, and is the only one that can say what was believed at a past tick; asOf(year) re-seeds the clock and evaluates once, has no history at all, but can be asked ANY question at ANY year - they agreed on 142 domain facts byte-identical, which is what licenses using the cheap one for the semiring folds (see f_counting_breaks_under_ticks); this needs to reach LIMITS.md, because more than one spec in the set assumes the stronger reading").
addressed_by(f_past_tick_is_provenance_only, "examples/sus/").

finding(f_admission_test_near_its_own_timeout, pitfall).
recorded(f_admission_test_near_its_own_timeout, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_admission_test_near_its_own_timeout, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_admission_test_near_its_own_timeout,
  "pre-existing and not caused by any work this session: test/admission.test.ts takes 30 SECONDS on a quiet machine under node and was measured at ~70 s by a demo agent while three agents shared the box, against the 60-second per-test timeout the project's own bun script sets - so the margin is a factor of two and any parallel load eats it, which is how it flaked in that agent's bun run; two separate consequences: it is a latent CI flake now that bun is CI-only, and at 30 s it is a QUARTER of the entire 117-second node suite in a single file, which is the largest single lever on the check-run budget if that budget ever tightens again").

finding(f_silent_pack_is_the_same_hole_thrice, insight).
recorded(f_silent_pack_is_the_same_hole_thrice, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_silent_pack_is_the_same_hole_thrice, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_silent_pack_is_the_same_hole_thrice,
  "LOOT names 'the mute pack' the WORST of its eight poisonings - a rule pack requiring predicates the extractor does not produce derives nothing, and silence is indistinguishable from 'no threats found'; that is the SAME hole found independently from three directions on the same day: (1) the kernel mechanism recorded this morning as f_unknown_relation_silently_empty, where contains(S,\"404\") loads clean because an unknown lowercase functor in a body position silently becomes an unpopulatable relation - the mute pack at the granularity of a single rule; (2) the top level's own failure class recorded as RULE 2 after four instances in one session, where an empty tool result was read as a fact about the subject rather than about the instrument; (3) LOOT's spec, written without knowledge of either; the convergence matters because the FIX is already specified and is one artefact serving all three: undefined_premise[audit](R, Rel) as a boot.rofl rule - edb, concludes and premise_pos are all already in the store, so it needs no kernel code - and that same audit IS the dictionary-compatibility check LOOT demands as a precondition for installing a pack; build it once, three problems close").
addressed_by(f_silent_pack_is_the_same_hole_thrice, "test/undefined-premise.test.ts").

finding(f_ring_composition_has_one_measurement, insight).
recorded(f_ring_composition_has_one_measurement, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_ring_composition_has_one_measurement, doc).
finding_note(f_ring_composition_has_one_measurement,
  "the set's README states the composition requirement in falsifiable form - 'ring 0 is useful on its own, each next ring is built WITHOUT REWORKING the previous; if the fourth requires rewriting the first, we need to know now' - and this session produced its first measurement, in favour: the semiring layer was deliberately designed as a FOLD OVER THE EXISTING support hypergraph rather than a second evaluator, precisely so the Boolean fixpoint would not be touched, and the golden capture of why-trees, canonicalState and snapshot round-trips came back BYTE-IDENTICAL (461489 bytes) twice - once after the witness-forest change and again after recursive whynot; so a ring was added twice without perturbing a byte of the previous one, n=1 architecture and 2 changes, which is evidence and not proof; SEPARATE CRITIQUE of the README's falsifying experiment, which as designed tests the wrong half: it proposes rebuilding HUH or NOPE on Souffle-with-provenance over Datomic and treating a one-week success as proof that ROFL is a library rather than a substrate - but HUH and NOPE are pure fixpoint plus derivation tree, exactly what Souffle already has, so succeeding would merely confirm what the repo's own landscape doc already concluded ('the discipline is the product, the fixpoint is a commodity'); to actually falsify, port SUS or LOOT - the ones needing asserted_by-scoped coexisting contradictions and pack-level provenance for an untrusted writer, which is the half no Datalog performance engine models at all").

finding(f_goof_on_real_mathlib, idea).
recorded(f_goof_on_real_mathlib, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_goof_on_real_mathlib, decision).
finding_note(f_goof_on_real_mathlib,
  "Vadim's proposal, and it beats GOOF.md's own plan: instead of hand-formalising plane geometry, map Lean/Mathlib and ask which theorems transitively depend on which axiom - the parallel-postulate story on real mathematics; GROUNDED, not speculated: ~/mathlib4 exists with mathlib-v5.jsonl, a 1.5 GB 14.16M-line graph export whose node types include THEOREM/DEFINITION/INSTANCE/AXIOM and whose edge types include PROOF_USES, TYPE_USES, VALUE_USES, CONTAINS, IMPORTS; the whole corpus has exactly FIFTEEN axiom nodes and the three that matter are there - choice (the classical-logic switch GOOF wants), propext, sound - plus sorryAx, which the spec never asked for and which yields a query nobody runs: which Mathlib theorems transitively rest on an admitted proof, i.e. OOPS machinery on a real corpus; THE SUBSTITUTION TO REFUSE: ROFL cannot CHECK Lean proofs and must never claim to - Lean's kernel does dependent type checking, ROFL is Datalog with stratified negation, so the honest claim is reasoning about DEPENDENCY STRUCTURE, and GOOF becomes 'one rule set, three axiom sets, which theorems survive' rather than 'three geometries'; ORACLE is free, exact and by construction rather than by parallel implementation: Lean's own #print axioms; FIRST STEP, unverified and cheap to settle: does the transitive closure over PROOF_USES + TYPE_USES actually reproduce #print axioms? Lean tracks dependencies over the full environment including types and instances, so take ~50 theorems on a slice and compare - agreement makes GOOF real, disagreement tells us what the model is missing and is equally a result; one export serves FOUR specs (GOOF axiom dependence, OOPS lemma-removal blast radius, DITTO refactor invariants, IFFY definition change) with free ground truth and no translation layer at all").

finding(f_storage_is_a_port_not_a_rewrite, insight).
recorded(f_storage_is_a_port_not_a_rewrite, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_storage_is_a_port_not_a_rewrite, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_storage_is_a_port_not_a_rewrite,
  "DECIDED by Vadim 2026-08-30, and it dissolves the section-8 conflict cleanly: the kernel stays small and toy, and instead of BUILDING a persistence layer it EXPOSES A PORT - a storage interface plus a mode flag - so a third-party DBMS can be plugged in by whoever actually needs scale; section 8 forbids building persistence, not having an interface someone else's persistence satisfies, and the zero-dependency property the landscape doc names as ROFL's occupied cell survives intact because the interface lives in src/ while every adapter lives outside it; MEASURED, the port is cheap because Store was already written as a narrow surface - 47 method calls against only 5 direct field accesses (store.facts twice in api.ts and once in semiring.ts, store.witnesses once each in engine.ts and api.ts), so the work is sealing those five leaks behind methods (an allFacts iterator, a witnessOf accessor), extracting the interface, and keeping the in-memory Store as the default; THE CONFORMANCE ORACLE IS FREE AND EXACT, which is the part that makes this safe: the in-memory store IS the reference implementation, so any adapter must produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL canonicalState() on the same program - mechanical, deterministic, and exactly the oracle shape this session settled on; four constraints any adapter must meet, all of them already implied by the kernel's determinism: relPersp/relAll must return canonically SORTED keys or canonicalState diverges and the determinism tests break; the seminaive front (matchPremise with an `only` key set) must stay an efficient lookup or semi-naive evaluation loses its point; the kernel must gain no dependency; and clone() is where a real backend BEATS the in-memory store rather than merely matching it, since an MVCC snapshot makes forks cheap and would settle f_fork_copies_the_whole_store as a side effect rather than as separate work; modes to support: memory (default, today's behaviour, zero-dep), and external (adapter), with spill-to-disk as a possible middle; WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: an adapter that cannot produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL canonicalState on the same program, because the whole safety of the port rests on the in-memory store being an exact conformance oracle").

finding(f_engine_needs_a_storage_tier, idea).
recorded(f_engine_needs_a_storage_tier, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_engine_needs_a_storage_tier, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_engine_needs_a_storage_tier,
  "Mathlib turns the store's scaling limit from a synthetic benchmark into a corpus with a reason: the export is ~806k nodes and ~13.4M edges (extrapolated from 22762 nodes / 377238 edges per 400k lines), while the measured store does 200k facts in one relation in 22.7 SECONDS and is O(n^2) because every add() does a binary search plus Array.splice - so 13.4M facts extrapolates to roughly 28 HOURS plus multi-gigabyte memory; the full corpus does not fit, and 'does not fit' is different from 'slow'; the work this names is a storage tier, not a micro-optimisation - spill to disk, incremental maintenance, separating compute from storage, and an external DBMS or an existing Datalog backend as the endpoint; NOTE THE SPEC COLLISION, this is not a free optimisation: START.md section 8 declares BOTH 'no incremental maintenance' AND 'no persistence layers' out of scope, and docs/formal-reasoning-landscape.md defines ROFL's occupied cell as 'a zero-dependency kernel small enough to live inside an agent harness' - an external DBMS trades exactly that property away, so it needs a decision, not a patch; the same landscape doc already wrote the contingency though, and it reads as if for this moment: the Datalog performance family (Souffle/Doop, DDlog/Differential Dataflow/Materialize, Glean/Kythe, CodeQL) are 'potential BACKENDS, not competitors: if scale ever hurts, compile the rule packs down to them; the discipline is the product, the fixpoint is a commodity' - scale now hurts, measurably, on a named corpus, so the contingency is triggered rather than invented; related and cheaper first steps already recorded: f_store_index_insert_quadratic (the index itself) and f_fork_copies_the_whole_store (clone via JSON round-trip, which IFFY and DITTO need cheap)").

finding(f_heck_tests_the_discipline_contract, question).
recorded(f_heck_tests_the_discipline_contract, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_heck_tests_the_discipline_contract, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_heck_tests_the_discipline_contract,
  "HECK (contradiction as the objective function: carrier = a measure of chaos, plus = max, times = addition, and an approved change must INCREASE contradictoriness) converges by a mechanism no current instance uses - it is BOUNDED ABOVE rather than non-increasing, because a finite predicate vocabulary bounds the number of distinguishable contradictions; every BOUNDED instance shipped so far (boolean, tropical with non-negative weights) converges because values do not grow, so if the BOUNDED discipline was implemented assuming non-growth rather than bounded height, HECK breaks it; this makes HECK the best available test that the convergence contract was specified correctly rather than fitted to the four familiar cases, which is exactly the failure mode the break-new-abstractions discipline warns about - queue it as ENGINE work alongside Viterbi, not as a demo, because its value is the contract test first and the joke second; the spec asks for separate property tests on this convergence mechanism, and it is right to").

finding(f_bleep_and_blam_are_cheaper_than_the_first_wave, idea).
recorded(f_bleep_and_blam_are_cheaper_than_the_first_wave, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_bleep_and_blam_are_cheaper_than_the_first_wave, decision).
finding_note(f_bleep_and_blam_are_cheaper_than_the_first_wave,
  "of the five specs added late (BLAM, DRIP, BLEEP, GOOF, HECK), two are cheaper than the three demos already in flight: BLEEP is a four-element lattice clean/dubious/dirty/forbidden with times=min and plus=max, BOUNDED by construction (height 3) so it converges on cyclic data for free - one semiring instance plus a small demo, and it EXTENDS THE INSTANCE SET rather than only adding a demo; BLAM is a toy build graph, acyclic by construction so counting is safe without star, and its own spec demands a code comment saying so precisely to stop the pattern being copied where cycles exist, which is the discipline we adopted this session appearing independently in a spec; DRIP separately satisfies the monkey-argument oracle criterion better than any other example because extraction is deterministic at BOTH ends (dashboards are JSON, metrics parse out of query text, live series come from the store) so the translation layer that sinks JOPA needs neither a model nor an expert, and ground truth is free - the prediction 'this panel is empty' is checked by opening the panel; GOOF is the most beautiful and the most expensive, formalising plane geometry is real work; DECISION: run BLEEP and BLAM as a second wave after the first three land, rather than five agents at once").
addressed_by(f_bleep_and_blam_are_cheaper_than_the_first_wave, "examples/bleep/").
addressed_by(f_bleep_and_blam_are_cheaper_than_the_first_wave, "examples/blam/").

finding(f_semiring_convergence_is_instance_contract, insight).
recorded(f_semiring_convergence_is_instance_contract, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_semiring_convergence_is_instance_contract, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_semiring_convergence_is_instance_contract,
  "Vadim's CORRECTIONS-semiring-convergence note independently reached f_semiring_table_lacks_closure and put it in a stronger place: the requirement belongs to the KERNEL's semiring-instance contract, not to the example specs; every instance must declare exactly one of BOUNDED (chain stabilises - Boolean, tropical with non-negative weights), CLOSED (supplies star(x) AND 'infinitely many' is a legal carrier value, not an overflow - so Counting on a cycle honestly answers 'infinite', which is CORRECT rather than a failure), or BOUNDED-UNFOLDING (counted to a declared depth or over simple paths, which CHANGES the semantics and must be documented, never silent); omega-continuity must be property-tested alongside associativity and distributivity because the semiring LAWS are exactly what does not break here; two side notes worth keeping: the joke AAAAA instance is BOUNDED and therefore safer than two of the serious ones, and access-control counting wants simple paths specifically because re-entering a role in an assume-role cycle grants no new privilege; correction was sent to the in-flight implementation agent").

finding(f_spat_is_the_cheap_afk, insight).
recorded(f_spat_is_the_cheap_afk, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_spat_is_the_cheap_afk, demo).
finding_note(f_spat_is_the_cheap_afk,
  "Vadim arrived at AFK's thesis from the domestic side, asking whether the schedule model should live on a server and ping when it sees a divergence - that IS AFK's standing-queries-that-fire-themselves, and the pairing dissolves AFK's recorded blocker: f_afk_needs_incremental_maintenance says the environment degrades quadratically because every write re-runs the fixpoint over a growing store, but a family week is a few hundred facts and changes a few times a DAY, while the O(n^2) insert only bites in the hundreds of thousands - so SPAT is the AFK instance that passes on today's kernel, where AFK's own infra-monitoring setting is killed by write rate; the design content is in WHAT to ping about, and the answer is not 'changes': pinging on every calendar edit is noise that gets muted within a week, so the standing queries must fire on DERIVED predicates - a child becomes uncovered, a chain's slack reaches zero or negative, a responsibility's backup count falls to one or zero, the two calendars contradict each other on the same slot under different asserted_by, or an external constraint moved and transitively marked what rested on it; each of those already exists in the model; and the ping MUST carry the whynot tree rather than a summary, because 'a child uncovered Thu 18:00-20:30 - the employer until 20:30 (owner: work), swimming until 18:00 (owner: the other adult), no nanny Thursday (owner: nanny)' is the only thing that distinguishes it from every calendar notification ever shipped; sequencing judgement: the VALUE is the standing-query set, not the daemon, and the queries are available today without any server - a daemon only adds 'you find out without asking', which is a real but LAST increment").

finding(f_afk_needs_incremental_maintenance, question).
recorded(f_afk_needs_incremental_maintenance, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_afk_needs_incremental_maintenance, demo).
finding_note(f_afk_needs_incremental_maintenance,
  "AFK is the only example that is a RUNTIME demonstration rather than a query, and it collides with two measured limits at once: LIMITS.md says any base-fact change discards the derived layer and re-runs the fixpoint from scratch (no incremental maintenance, declared out of scope by START.md section 8), while the store index insert is O(n^2) (measured 10k/45ms, 50k/543ms, 200k/22.7s); AFK requires several agents writing asynchronously into one store over a multi-hour run, so every single assertion triggers a full re-evaluation over a store that is itself growing - the environment gets quadratically slower with every write, which is precisely the property an environment must not have; AFK's other needs are cheaper than they look (contradictions by asserted_by and transitive at_risk are native; standing queries and effect veto are runtime-layer work over existing tick boundaries and why-trees, not kernel work); decision needed on whether AFK forces incremental maintenance back into scope or whether it ships with a declared write-rate ceiling").

finding(f_a_stratum_is_earned_by_negation, insight).
recorded(f_a_stratum_is_earned_by_negation, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_a_stratum_is_earned_by_negation, doc).
finding_note(f_a_stratum_is_earned_by_negation,
  "the top level briefed the WTF agent that MTG's seven layers ARE stratification and that boot.rofl computes the order from the dependency graph - that was an overclaim, caught by Vadim asking for an explanation, and measured before it reached a README: encoding the layer as an ARGUMENT is rejected outright (unstratified, the same relation-level limitation as RIP), while encoding each layer as its own RELATION loads but does NOT automatically produce ascending strata; the corrected statement is that a kernel stratum is a level forced by NEGATION and derived by boot.rofl, whereas an MTG layer is an order ASSERTED by the Comprehensive Rules, so they coincide only if the encoding makes layer N's rules negate on layer N-1's relation - an obligation the modeller constructs deliberately, not a free property of the domain; the WTF demo then did better than accept the correction, it PROVED it with a discriminating test - the layer boundaries that are strata are exactly the ones carrying a removal, and routing the negation off the layer below makes the stratum vanish and changes nothing else; the general form worth carrying to every other example: a stratum is EARNED BY NEGATION, never by intent or by documentation, and the way to show a program's order was derived rather than scheduled is to print the observed stratum/2 values and to demonstrate that removing the negation removes the level").
addressed_by(f_a_stratum_is_earned_by_negation, "examples/wtf/").

finding(f_stratification_is_relation_level, insight).
recorded(f_stratification_is_relation_level, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_stratification_is_relation_level, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_stratification_is_relation_level,
  "RIP needs an AND/OR alternating fixpoint and its third category ('not derivable' = depends on luck) is literally the unknown of well-founded semantics; measured, three encodings: the textbook game encoding (win :- move, lose; lose :- state, not has_win_move) is rejected unstratified AS EXPECTED, but the STEP-INDEXED encoding is rejected TOO even though it is provably sound (the index strictly decreases, so no ground derivation cycles through negation) - because boot.rofl's unstratified/1 works on the relation dependency graph and knows nothing about argument positions, i.e. the kernel has no LOCAL stratification; what does load today is manual unrolling of each horizon into DISTINCT relations (win0/lose0/win1/lose1/...), verified running, but that is bounded lookahead written out by hand N times and only approximates the WFS third value; decision needed between argument-indexed (local) stratification and a real alternating fixpoint - the latter is what RIP actually asks for and is a much larger change than semirings").

finding(f_universal_needs_suffix_indexing, insight).
recorded(f_universal_needs_suffix_indexing, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_universal_needs_suffix_indexing, doc).
finding_note(f_universal_needs_suffix_indexing,
  "HONK's all(I in Ingredients: craftable(I)) has exactly one encoding that survives a cyclic graph: the naive monotone cons-list walk enumerates every list over every craftable item (99747 junk facts, budget dead), the demand-driven form diverges because demand evaluation has no tabling, the negation form is correctly rejected as unstratified - only the suffix-indexed walk (suffix(T) :- suffix(cons(_,T)), ok ranging over recipe suffixes) runs, in 1ms with 8 facts and a full why-tree; the gap between the sugar and the surviving encoding IS the example's lesson and must be shown, not hidden").

finding(f_semiring_needs_parameterized_evaluator, idea).
recorded(f_semiring_needs_parameterized_evaluator, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_semiring_needs_parameterized_evaluator, decision).
finding_note(f_semiring_needs_parameterized_evaluator,
  "HONK's seven semirings need Evaluation<S>: the value lattice is hard-wired to the powerset of ground facts (Store.add is present/absent, conclude has no annotation slot) and in-language simulation fails - tropical (min,+) over the mandated cycle derived 74831 cost facts enumerating every path length before the budget stopped it; a semiring interface is generic so src/ is where it legally belongs under the kernel grep test, BUT START.md section 8 lists aggregation under do-not-build and semiring evaluation is generalized aggregation, so this is a spec amendment to decide before building; roadmap Phase 6 anticipates it ('Later Model: use semiring annotations') without scoping it").

finding(f_semiring_table_lacks_closure, question).
recorded(f_semiring_table_lacks_closure, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_semiring_table_lacks_closure, decision).
finding_note(f_semiring_table_lacks_closure,
  "HONK.md's table gives seven rows of oplus/otimes and no closure column, but on the cyclic graphs the spec MANDATES you also need 0-bar, 1-bar and a star operator: Boolean/Polynomial/Best-derivation are oplus-idempotent so Kleene iteration terminates, Counting over a cycle has infinitely many derivation trees (undefined, not merely slow), Tropical needs a*=0-bar or Dijkstra ordering; AAAAA's 'did not converge' is the honest symptom but the other six are supposed to CONVERGE - fix the spec before implementing it").

finding(f_whynot_is_depth_one, pitfall).
recorded(f_whynot_is_depth_one, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_whynot_is_depth_one, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_whynot_is_depth_one,
  "whynot names the failing premise and stops: on a four-stage pipe, whynot s_sort(4,checkout) reports 'failed premise: s_awk(4,checkout)' and never reaches the builtin 404<=499 that actually dropped the line - but 'which stage ate it' IS HUH's entire selling point; LIMITS.md's 'whynot is single-step' is about multi-fact ABDUCTION, a different axis, since recursing the tree stays single-step per level, so this needs no section-8 amendment; machinery (whynotStruct + matchPremise) already exists, needs a cycle guard and a depth budget - cheapest high-value item found").
addressed_by(f_whynot_is_depth_one, "src/api.ts").

finding(f_witness_forest_dropped, pitfall).
recorded(f_witness_forest_dropped, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_witness_forest_dropped, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_witness_forest_dropped,
  "Store.support() accumulates every firing signature in firings but keeps only the FIRST structured Witness, so why renders one proof tree where three exist (verified: supportCount 3, one branch shown); Best-derivation, Polynomial, Counting and HUH's 'which 47 lines' all need the forest - good news that shrinks the estimate: the hypergraph IS retained since signatures are ruleId|fact:key|..., only the structured form is dropped, so Map<string, Witness[]> is a few-line change and the real work is the output contract (why rendering ONE tree stays the right default; this needs a forest mode, not a replacement)").

finding(f_unknown_relation_silently_empty, pitfall).
recorded(f_unknown_relation_silently_empty, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_unknown_relation_silently_empty, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_unknown_relation_silently_empty,
  "hit(N) :- msg(N,S), contains(S,\"404\"). LOADS CLEAN: contains is not a builtin so it parses as a relation with no rules and no facts, and hit is silently always empty - every typo'd builtin becomes a silently-empty relation; unmoded[audit] catches unknown BUILTINS, nothing catches an unpopulatable body relation; costs nothing to fix because edb/concludes/premise_pos are already in the store, so undefined_premise[audit](R,Rel) is a boot.rofl rule and not kernel code").
addressed_by(f_unknown_relation_silently_empty, "boot.rofl").

finding(f_no_string_builtins_moves_why_boundary, question).
recorded(f_no_string_builtins_moves_why_boundary, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_no_string_builtins_moves_why_boundary, decision).
finding_note(f_no_string_builtins_moves_why_boundary,
  "there are no string builtins at all and 'is' is integer-only (3/2 = 1, so Viterbi has no carrier either), so a pipe demo whose stages are grep and awk must push tokenization into the host - architecturally fine, that is what scanners/ is for, but it MOVES THE WHY-BOUNDARY: why then explains from tokenized facts onward while HUH's spec tree starts at 'grep 4xx passed 47 of 128340'; decide deliberately between mode-checked string builtins and a redrawn demo boundary, do not let it happen by accident").

finding(f_acceptance_must_include_the_full_suite, pitfall).
recorded(f_acceptance_must_include_the_full_suite, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_acceptance_must_include_the_full_suite,
  "the three-step acceptance ritual installed earlier today - verify, settle the finding, stop the worker - was run on the meta-layer cache and PASSED EVERYTHING IT CHECKED while the change was broken: the agent's own 15 tests green, the byte-exact goldens moved ZERO bytes, examples/spat went 29.8 s to 6.4 s with re-evaluation at 291 ms; then the full node suite came back 291 pass / 9 FAIL, every failure in examples/wtf, which had passed 19 of 19 an hour earlier, with a creature's characteristics computed as 2/2 where an independent implementation of the Magic layer rules says 3/3 - wrong answers, not flakes; WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT: examples/wtf carries 14 distinct stratum levels over 193 relations while every other program here lives on one to three, so the goldens (craft, multi-support, sensors) and the agent's own fixtures were all too shallow, and the engine activates negation rules stratum by stratum to fixpoint, which is precisely the sequencing a reused derived layer has to respect; THE CORRECTION TO THE RITUAL, which matters more than the bug: 'verify behaviour' for a change inside src/ MUST include the full suite BEFORE the finding is settled and the worker stopped, because goldens are a regression net for the programs they cover and nothing else - I settled two findings and stopped the agent on evidence that was strictly weaker than the evidence I already had available, and had to reopen both; the deeper lesson is that a golden is not a substitute for coverage, it is a substitute for a diff").
addressed_by(f_acceptance_must_include_the_full_suite, "CLAUDE.md").

finding(f_materialise_the_meta_layer, idea).
recorded(f_materialise_the_meta_layer, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_materialise_the_meta_layer, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_materialise_the_meta_layer,
  "QUEUED by Vadim 2026-08-30 as the next kernel task after WTF and MOOT land: cache boot.rofl's meta-layer instead of rebuilding it on every load, with an EXACT invalidation key rather than a heuristic; the measurement that makes this cheap - four kinds of data change were applied to a loaded program and the meta relations watched: adding a fact of an EXISTING relation moved NOTHING (dep 37, reach 53, stratum 43 unchanged); adding a fact of a NEW relation moved only stratum (43->44); a fact in a NEW perspective moved stratum and sees/perspective (44->45, 2->3); a fact WITH an author fired forged (0->1); THE RESULT IS THAT THE EXPENSIVE PART HAS THE CHEAPEST KEY - dep, dep_neg and reach are completely immune to data and change only when RULES change, and reach is precisely the 1799 facts costing ~15 s in SPAT, so its key is just the set of rule ids, which are already content-addressed (r + fnv1a of the canonical clause); stratum needs a slightly wider key (rule ids + the set of relations carrying an edb mark + the set of perspectives) because it is sensitive to the FIRST APPEARANCE of a relation or perspective though not to fact volume; forged and leak are genuinely per-fact and not cacheable, but they cost nothing; DO NOT be tempted to compute strata in the host by an ordinary graph pass, which is what every production Datalog does - START.md states that the kernel deliberately contains no stratification checker and that boot computes it AS DATA, which is the demonstrable property the whole design exists for, so caching preserves the discipline while reimplementation destroys it; ACCEPTANCE, without which this should not be built: a permanent test asserting cached == uncached BYTE-IDENTICAL on canonicalState(), in the same form as the existing incremental-equals-scratch claim in LIMITS.md, PLUS a negative test that mutates a rule and proves the cache MISSED rather than serving a stale answer - a cache is where correctness goes to die and a gate that has never said no is indistinguishable from an absent one").
addressed_by(f_materialise_the_meta_layer, "test/derived-reuse.test.ts").

finding(f_boot_meta_layer_costs_with_rule_count, pitfall).
recorded(f_boot_meta_layer_costs_with_rule_count, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_boot_meta_layer_costs_with_rule_count, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_boot_meta_layer_costs_with_rule_count,
  "the SPAT example is the first program here large enough to make boot.rofl's OWN audit machinery expensive, and the cost splits almost exactly in half - measured on the same model: with boot.rofl loaded, 29.8 s and 16032 facts of which reach=1799 and derived_by=6940; without boot.rofl, 15.1 s and 13516 facts with reach=0; so roughly FIFTEEN SECONDS is the meta-layer alone, and it is boot's transitive closure reach(A,B) :- reach(A,X), dep(X,B) over the RELATION dependency graph, which is quadratic in the number of relations a program declares - every audit boot performs (malformed, breach, leak, forged, unmoded, undefined_premise, unstratified) rides on that closure, so the price of self-auditing grows with the size of the audited program rather than with its data; note this is a cost of the DISCIPLINE, not of the domain - the same rules over the same week without boot cost half as much and lose every audit - so the decision is not 'make reach faster' but 'when is the closure recomputed': it is currently rebuilt from scratch on every load, while the rule set changes far less often than the data does, which points at caching the meta-layer across loads rather than at optimising the closure itself; a correction to my own first reading, recorded because the reading was wrong in the displacing way: I said 'the blow-up is not the model, it is the meta-layer' when the measurement says BOTH, half each - the domain model materialises 13516 facts from a fine-grained time grid and owns its own 15 seconds").
addressed_by(f_boot_meta_layer_costs_with_rule_count, "src/engine.ts").

finding(f_the_quadratic_is_arrival_order, insight).
recorded(f_the_quadratic_is_arrival_order, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_the_quadratic_is_arrival_order, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_the_quadratic_is_arrival_order,
  "corrects f_store_index_insert_quadratic, which blamed the data structure: the quadratic is not a property of the sorted-array index, it is a property of the ORDER keys arrive in - measured at 64000 facts, three arrival orders into the same index: strictly ascending keys 132 ms (2 us/fact), integer arguments whose lexicographic order is scrambled 505 ms (8 us/fact), zero-padded but shuffled 716 ms (11 us/fact), a 5.4x spread on identical data; the mechanism is that Array.splice at the END is free while splice into the MIDDLE is a memmove of the whole tail, and this explains a result that looked contradictory - store.clone() is LINEAR at 3-4 us/fact because restore() replays a snapshot whose facts are already sorted, so every insert appends; the consequence that matters: DERIVED facts arrive in rule-firing order, never in key order, so the engine is permanently in the bad case for exactly the layer it generates most of; the fix is therefore cheaper than a data-structure replacement - buffer a round's derivations and merge them sorted, or keep an unsorted append list plus a sorted view rebuilt per round, rather than replacing the index wholesale").
addressed_by(f_the_quadratic_is_arrival_order, "src/store.ts").

finding(f_provenance_is_a_dag_not_a_tree, insight).
recorded(f_provenance_is_a_dag_not_a_tree, "2026-08-30").
finding_note(f_provenance_is_a_dag_not_a_tree,
  "the comfortable excuse 'we are slow because we keep derivation trees' is FALSE and now measured both ways; Souffle's provenance paper measured the naive direct encoding - every tuple annotated with its full subproof - at UP TO 100x MEMORY on transitive closure at 2000 tuples, and abandoned it; ROFL's Witness.prems holds the direct children of a derivation, which LOOKS like exactly that design, so it was checked on graphs of rising density: witnesses per answer came out 1.00 on a path, 1.93 then 1.97 on a 2-out DAG, 2.80 then 2.90 on a 3-out DAG - the ratio equals the BRANCHING FACTOR and does not grow with n; the reason is that store.support() deduplicates by firing signature and premises are referenced BY KEY, so what is stored is a support HYPERGRAPH with one edge per distinct derivation, not a proof TREE in which every subtree is repeated and the size explodes; by construction ROFL therefore sits with ProvSQL's shared-circuit design (2-4x measured) rather than with the abandoned encoding, and within one order of Souffle's constant-witness design (1.27x time, 1.45x space, achieved by storing only a rule id and a minimal proof height and reconstructing trees on demand); TWO INVARIANTS FOLLOW - provenance is linear in derivations and never exponential, and any future claim that a performance problem is 'the cost of provenance' must be measured against that before it is believed; the upgrade path exists if it ever does become the constraint, but it trades why from a lookup into a re-derivation, and a recorded witness is a record of what happened while a reconstruction is only a plausible account of it - not a trade worth making to save 1.5x").
addressed_by(f_provenance_is_a_dag_not_a_tree, "docs/performance-invariants.md").

finding(f_no_argument_index_is_the_bottleneck, insight).
recorded(f_no_argument_index_is_the_bottleneck, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_no_argument_index_is_the_bottleneck, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_no_argument_index_is_the_bottleneck,
  "a CPU profile of the realistic workload, taken instead of guessing at the next axis, re-ranked the whole kernel queue: matchPremise 43.6 percent of self time, unify 18.3, garbage collector 11.6, store.get 8.1, and store.add ONLY 2.6 - so insertion, which had just been optimised by 40x on its isolated axis, is now 2.6 percent of the cost while PREMISE MATCHING AND UNIFICATION ARE 62 PERCENT; the mechanism is in matchPremise: for a positive premise it takes relPersp(rel, persp) - EVERY fact of that relation in that perspective - and runs unify over each candidate's arguments, so the store is indexed by (relation, perspective) and by NOTHING ELSE, and every positive premise is a full relation scan filtered by unification; concretely, peer(A,B) :- item(A,G), item(B,G), A != B over 500 items makes the second premise scan all 500 for each of the 500 bindings of the first, a quarter of a million unify calls where an index on the second argument would visit the ten members of that group; this is precisely the problem Souffle's automatic index selection solves - a minimum-chain-cover over the primitive searches a program performs, computed at compile time - and it is worth more here than the 'up to 2x and 6x less memory' that paper reports, because they were choosing BETWEEN index sets while this engine has no argument indexes at all; NOTE THE METHOD, which is the transferable part: the previous ranking put compact fact representation next on the strength of a 40-70x memory gap against Souffle, an ISOLATED AXIS argument of exactly the kind that had just been shown to overpromise - buffer-and-merge delivered 40x on its own axis and 2x end to end - so the profile was taken first and it named a different item entirely").

finding(f_memory_per_fact_is_the_real_ceiling, insight).
recorded(f_memory_per_fact_is_the_real_ceiling, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_memory_per_fact_is_the_real_ceiling, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_memory_per_fact_is_the_real_ceiling,
  "the widest gap to the field is not speed and not provenance, it is BYTES PER FACT: measured 861 MB at 610309 facts is about 0.7M facts per GB, against roughly 30-50M tuples per GB derived from Souffle's published Doop tables (595-1124 MB for 13-27M IDB tuples) - a 40-70x gap, and it is structural: a JavaScript object graph with string keys against a compiled B-tree of small-arity integer tuples; consequences that follow directly - a 13.4M-edge corpus like the Mathlib export does not fit and no index tuning closes that, and the techniques that buy the field its numbers are all PREMATURE here since automatic index selection is worth up to 2x and 6x less memory than maximal indexing, a well-engineered interpreter is only 2-6x off generated C++, and parallelism yields under 25 percent CPU utilisation on long-tail workloads - none of them touches a 40x representation gap; the ordered work is therefore buffer-and-merge derived insertions (up to 5.4x, contained), cache the meta-layer against the rule set (~15 s on a spat-sized program), copy-on-write forks, and THEN compact fact representation, which is where the order of magnitude actually lives - interning relation names and perspectives, and storing small-arity tuples as typed arrays rather than term objects; an external storage backend belongs after all of those, because it cannot rescue a representation that is 40x too heavy").

finding(f_measured_ceiling_and_throughput, insight).
recorded(f_measured_ceiling_and_throughput, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_measured_ceiling_and_throughput, doc).
finding_note(f_measured_ceiling_and_throughput,
  "the first systematic scaling curve for this kernel, five axes, all on one laptop under load so treat the absolute numbers as a ceiling not a floor; AXIS raw insert into one relation: flat ~5 us/fact to 32k facts, then a knee - 128k costs 57 us/fact (7.3 s); AXIS transitive closure over a path: 160 nodes and 12720 derived facts in 1.5 s; AXIS PROVENANCE WEIGHT IS EXACTLY 2x - for every answer fact the store holds one derived_by fact and one witness, which is a clean invariant and better than feared, a full derivation tree costs a doubling of storage rather than an order; AXIS boot.rofl's self-audit is quadratic in the number of RELATIONS a program declares - 80 relations give 3292 reach facts and 434 ms, which is the same effect f_boot_meta_layer_costs_with_rule_count measured from the other end; AXIS clone is linear at 3-4 us/fact (see f_the_quadratic_is_arrival_order for why); THE CEILING, on a realistic mix of base facts plus a self-join plus a recursive rule plus negation: 500 base facts derive 39809 total in 7.7 s at 39 MB, while 2000 base facts derive 610309 total in 286 SECONDS at 861 MB - four times the input for thirty-seven times the time; SEPARATE THE TWO CAUSES HONESTLY - the derived blow-up from 40k to 610k is the PROGRAM's doing, a self-join is quadratic by nature and any engine would derive the same 610k facts, whereas the per-fact cost degrading from 193 to 469 us is the ENGINE's; net throughput is therefore roughly 2000-5000 derived facts per second and it degrades with size").

finding(f_store_index_insert_quadratic, pitfall).
recorded(f_store_index_insert_quadratic, "2026-08-30").
demands(f_store_index_insert_quadratic, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_store_index_insert_quadratic,
  "measured store inserts into one relation: 10k facts 45ms, 50k 543ms, 200k 22732ms - 5x the facts cost 12x the time and 4x more cost 42x, because the per-(rel,persp) index keeps a sorted array and every add() does a binary search plus Array.splice (an O(n) memmove, O(n^2) overall); extrapolating, HUH's own acceptance criterion of a million-line log is ~10 minutes in one relation or ~90s sharded across six stage relations before any fixpoint work, both breaking CLAUDE.md's 300-second check budget on their own; fix the index (lazy sort on append, or a skip structure) BEFORE promising the million-line number").
addressed_by(f_store_index_insert_quadratic, "src/store.ts").


-- ------------------------------------------------------------- settled ---

